#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
paper_vocab.py: single-file, self-contained
================================================
Build a word-frequency vocabulary from a folder of academic PDFs, then use
it to suggest synonyms for a given word, ranked by how often each synonym
actually appears in *your* papers, not just generic thesaurus output.

This file is self-contained: it already has a vocabulary built from your
3 uploaded papers (SDT/intrinsic motivation, "Stochastic Parrots", and the
tirzepatide obesity trial) embedded inside it, compressed. You can use it
immediately with no other files needed. Re-run "build" any time you want
to replace that vocabulary with one built from your own PDFs.

REQUIREMENTS (one-time setup)
------------------------------
    pip install nltk pdfplumber --break-system-packages
    python3 -c "import nltk; nltk.download('wordnet'); nltk.download('stopwords'); nltk.download('omw-1.4')"

USAGE
-----
1) Get corpus-backed synonyms for a word (uses the embedded vocab by default):

    python3 paper_vocab.py suggest --word "utilize"

2) See the most common words/phrases in the embedded vocab:

    python3 paper_vocab.py top --n 40

3) Build a NEW vocabulary from your own folder of PDFs (overwrites the
   embedded one for the rest of this run, and saves it to disk so future
   runs of "suggest"/"top" use it automatically):

    python3 paper_vocab.py build --pdf-dir /path/to/pdfs

4) Enrich the CURRENT vocabulary with more papers, keeping what's already
   there (counts are summed; papers already counted are skipped):

    python3 paper_vocab.py build --pdf-dir /path/to/new_pdfs --merge

   After building, this file will look for a sibling "vocab.json" next to
   it first (if present) before falling back to the embedded vocabulary,
   so your custom corpus persists across runs without needing to touch
   this file again.
"""

